1 TITL King Ping of Zhou
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} King Ping of Zhou (before 771 BC - 720 BC) (ch. 周平王 zhōu píng wáng) or King P'ing of Chou was the thirteenth sovereign of the Chinese Zhou Dynasty and the first of Eastern Zhou Dynasty. He fled when his father died
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Family name Ji (姬 jī) in Chinese 
2 CONT
2 CONT Given name Yijiu (宜臼 yí jìu) in Chinese 
2 CONT
2 CONT Era name none 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father King You of Zhou 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother Daughter of Marquis of Shen 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife unknown 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandchildren King Huan of Zhou 
2 CONT
2 CONT Duration of reign 770 BC-720 BC 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomb unknown 
2 CONT
2 CONT Temple name unknown 
2 CONT
2 CONT Courtesy name unknown 
2 CONT
2 CONT Posthumous name Ping 平 (pinyin píng), literary meaning: "peaceful" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Ping_of_Zhou
2 CONT
2 CONT King Ping of Zhou (died 720 BC) (Chinese: 周平王; pinyin: Zhōu Píng Wáng), formerly romanized as King P’ing of Chou,[2] was the thirteenth king of the Chinese Zhou Dynasty and the first of Eastern Zhou Dynasty.[3] He was the son of K
2 CONC ing You of Zhou and Queen Shen, and was known as Crown Prince Yijiu before ascending the throne.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT King You had exiled Queen Shen and Yijiu after the king became enamoured with his concubine Bao Si and made her queen.[5] As a result, Queen Shen’s father, the Marquess of Shen, teamed with the Quanrong nomads and local satellite 
2 CONC states to overthrow King You.[6][7] You was killed, and Bao Si captured. Yijiu ascended the throne and was posthumously known as King Ping of Zhou.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Ping moved the Zhou Dynasty east from Haojing to Luoyang,[8] thus ending the Western Zhou[9] and beginning the Eastern Zhou Dynasty and the Spring and Autumn period.
2 CONT
2 CONT Over 14 centuries after King Ping’s death, Tang Dynasty Empress regnant Wu Zetian claimed ancestry from King Ping through his son Prince Wu, and changed the dynastic name to Zhou, which was reverted to Tang after her death.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT King Ping’s son was Xiefu, father of the King Huan of Zhou.
2 CONT
