1 TITL King You of Zhou
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Taken from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_You_of_Zhou
2 CONT
2 CONT King You of Zhou (795–771 BC) (Chinese: 周幽王; pinyin: Zhōu Yōu Wáng) was the twelfth king of the Chinese Zhou Dynasty and the last of Western Zhou Dynasty. He reigned from 781 to 771 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 780 BC, a major earthquake hit Guanzhong. A soothsayer named Bo Yangfu (伯陽甫) considered this an omen foretelling the destruction of the Zhou Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 779 BC, a concubine named Bao Si entered the palace and came into the King You's favour. She bore him a son named Bofu.[1] King You deposed Queen Shen (申后) and Crown Prince Yijiu. He made Baosi the new queen and Bofu the new cr
2 CONC own prince.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that Baosi did not laugh easily. After trying many methods and failing, King You tried to amuse his favourite queen by lighting warning beacons and fooling his nobles into thinking that the Quanrong nomads were about to
2 CONC  attack. The nobles arrived at the castle only to find themselves laughed at by Baosi. Even after King You had impressed Baosi, he continued to abuse his use of warning beacons and lost the trust of the nobles.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Shen’s father, the Marquess of Shen, was furious at the deposition of his daughter and grandson Crown Prince Yijiu and mounted an attack on King You's palace with the Quanrong. King You called for his nobles using the previo
2 CONC usly abused beacons but none came. In the end, King You and Bofu were killed and Baosi was captured.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT After King You died, nobles including the Marquess of Shen, the Marquess of Zeng (缯侯) and Duke Wen of Xu (許文公) supported deposed Prince Yijiu as King Ping of Zhou[5] to continue the Zhou Dynasty. As the national capital Haojing ha
2 CONC d suffered severe damage, and was located near the potentially dangerous Quanrong, in 771 BC, King Ping of Zhou moved the capital eastward to Luoyang, thus beginning the Eastern Zhou Dynasty and ushering in the Spring and Autumn p
2 CONC eriod which would last for more than 300 years.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT References[edit source]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Revised Chinese Dictionary, Ministry of Education, Taiwan 2.^ Sima Qian. Records of the Grand Historian 4. 3.^ Giles, Herbert A. (1912). The Civilization of China. Tutis Digital Publishing. ISBN 81-320-0448-5. Chapter 1 4.^ Ca
2 CONC mbridge History of Ancient China,1999, pages 546 and 551 5.^ Bamboo Annals 6.^ Phương Thi Danh (2001), Niên biểu lịch sử Trung Quốc
