1 OCCU calligrapher
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Wang Xizhi''' 王羲之 (303–361, alt. 321–379, 305–363, 307–365), ''zi'' '''Yishao''' 逸少. Eastern Jin writer and calligrapher.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Xizhi's ancestral home was Linyi 臨沂 in Langye 琅邪 commandery. His studio name was Danzhai 澹齋, and his childhood personal name was A'tu 阿菟. He is also known as Wang Youjun 王右軍, derived from his highest official title, Youjun ji
2 CONC angjun 右軍將軍 (general of the right army). Scholars have debated the year of Wang Xizhi's dates, but a good argument can be made for 303–361, for this is the date given in the commentary to the ''Zhen gao'' 真誥 of Tao Hongjing 陶弘景 (4
2 CONC 56–536).
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Xizhi's great-grandfather Wang Lan 王覽 (206–278) was the younger brother of the paragon of filial piety Wang Xiang 王祥 (185–269). His grandfather Wang Zheng 王正 was Wang Lan's fourth son. Wang Lan's oldest son Wang Cai 王裁 was th
2 CONC e father of the famous statesman Wang Dao 王導 (276–339). Wang Xizhi's father Wang Kuang 王曠 (fl. 305–318) was a supporter of Sima Rui 司馬睿 (276–322) when in 307 he established his regime south of the Yangzi. Wang Kuang' younger broth
2 CONC er Wang Yi 王廙 (276–322) was married to Sima Rui's sister. He was a famous painter and calligrapher and was one of Wang Xizhi's teachers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Wang Xizhi was young, he was clumsy in speech, but as he aged, he became quite skilled at oral argument and debate. At age thirteen he visited the eccentric celebrity Zhou Yi 周顗 (269–322), who was quite impressed with him. Wa
2 CONC ng Xizhi's father's cousin Wang Dun 王敦 (266– 324) and Wang Dao also regarded him highly. When Xi (or Chi) Jian 郗鑒 (269–339) was seeking a husband for his daughter Xi Xuan 郗璿 (d. 405), he selected Wang Xizhi. Xi Xuan was also a ski
2 CONC lled calligrapher. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Xizhi's first official position was assistant in the palace library ca. 324. Ca. 327, he became a you 友 (companion) of Sima Rui's youngest son Sima Yu 司馬昱 (320–372), Prince of Gui. In this capacity he was responsible for givi
2 CONC ng advice to the young prince, who was only seven at the time. Ca. 334, General Who Chastizes the West, Yu Liang 庾亮 (289– 340), appointed Wang Xizhi his military aide. In 336, he was assigned as governor of Linchuan 臨川 (modern Lin
2 CONC chuan, Jiangxi). Ca. 338, Wang Dao repeatedly invited Wang Xizhi to take the posts of palace attendant and director of the Department of Personnel, but Wang refused to serve. In 340, when Yu Liang was on his deathbed, he recommend
2 CONC ed Wang Xizhi as “pure and noble, a man with discriminating judgment.” Ca. 342, Wang Xizhi was named General Who Brings Repose to the Distance and regional inspector of Jiangzhou 江州 (administrative seat Xunyang 尋陽, southeast of mo
2 CONC dern Huangmei 黃梅, Hubei). In 346, he was apppointed General Who Defends the Army, but delayed quite some time before he took up his post. He only did so after urging from his friend Yin Hao 殷浩 (306–356). Wishing to serve as a loca
2 CONC l official, Wang Xizhi requested to be assigned as administrator of Xuancheng 宣城 (modern Xuancheng, Anhui). This request was not granted, but he was instead named General of the Right Army and appointed administrator of Guiji. In 
2 CONC 352, Yin Hao, who had now become general of the central army, led an expedition to recover the north to thwart the ambitions of Huan Wen 桓溫 (312–373). Wang Xizhi wrote a letter to Yin urging him to combine forces with Huan Wen, bu
2 CONC t Yin did not heed his advice. His expedition was a failure. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the third day of the third lunar month of 353, Wang Xizhi hosted a gathering of prominent men at Lan ting 蘭亭 post station in Shanyin 山陰 (modern Shaoxing). Each person was required to compose a poem. Those who could not complete
2 CONC  a poem had to drink a penalty toast. Wang Xizhi's preface that he composed for the occasion is one of the most famous pieces of Chinese calligraphy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 355, Wang Xizhi went to his parent's grave to announce that he was quitting official service for good. The immediate cause for his decision was having to serve under his nemesis Wang Shu 王述 (303–368), who had just been appointe
2 CONC d regional inspector of Yangzhou 揚州, which had jurisdiction over Guiji where Wang Xizhi was serving as governor. Not being able to bear the prospect of working under Wang Shu, Wang Xizhi decided to resign his post. He never served
2 CONC  in office again. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Xizhi spent his remaining years in Jinting 金庭 of Shan 剡 county (modern Sheng 剩 county, Zhejiang), where he enjoyed sightseeing, fishing, and gathering medicinal herbs. He died in 361 at the age of fifty-nine. He had at least 
2 CONC seven sons and two daughters. His second son Wang Ningzhi 王凝之 (d. 399) was a skilled calligrapher and poet. Ningzhi was married to Xie Daoyun 謝道韞 (before 340–after 399), the niece of Xie An 謝安 (320–385). She was a well known poet 
2 CONC and calligrapher. Wang Xizhi's most distinguished progeny was his seventh son Wang Xianzhi 王獻之 (344–385). One of Wang Xizhi's daughters was married to Xie Huan 謝瑍, the father of Xie Lingyun 謝靈運 (385–433). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Xizhi is commonly regarded as the foremost calligrapher in Chinese history. In addition to the preface that he composed for the Lan ting gathering, Wang Xizhi is credited with almost 700 notes (''shu tie'' 書帖), many of which 
2 CONC may be in his original hand. Most of the notes are addressed to relatives and friends. They include letters of recommendation and condolence, requests for medical cures and herbs, inquiries about another person's illness, and than
2 CONC k-you notes for presents. One famous piece is a lament about the destruction of the Wang family tombs. A substantial number of notes are complaints about Wang's own illnesses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Wang Xizhi's collected works in nine ''juan''. It also mentions a collection in a Liang period catalogue in ten ''juan'' with a table of contents in one ''juan''. The two Tang
2 CONC  histories record a collection in five ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Received versions of his collection are reconstructions done in the Ming.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [https://cbdb.fas.harvard.edu/cbdbapi/person.php?id=25793 '''Wang Xizhi''' 王羲之] [25793] Yan lu, 3.4a-4b; Giles, pp. 821-2; Zhou Jiayou, 1.98. QTW, 397.21a-26a (funerary inscription drafted by descendant, Shiqian 師乾, which includes
2 CONC  the names of some descendants whose addresses and dates are obscure). — [https://www.geni.com/projects/CBDB-Hartwell-collection/46406 RMH]
