2 CONT
2 CONT Yi Kung of Yan (燕易王) was a ruler of the State of Yan (燕国) during ancient China. He was the son of Wen Kung of Yan (文公) and the father of Hui Kung of Yan (惠公). His reign took place during the Warring States period (475–221 BCE), a 
2 CONC time of intense political and military conflict among various states.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Yi of Yan ruled as Duke of Yan from 331 BCE to 323 BCE, then promoted himself to King in 323 BCE. During his reign, Yan was attacked by the State of Qi, losing ten cities. However, he successfully sent Su Qin, a famous strate
2 CONC gist, to negotiate with Qi, leading to the return of those cities. His rule was heavily influenced by Su Qin, who controlled much of the court’s affairs in his final years.
