1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Xiao Zike''' 蕭子恪 (478–529), ''zi'' '''Jingchong''' 景冲. Qi and Liang period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Zike's ancestral home was Nan Lanling 南蘭陵 (northwest of modern Changzhou, Jiangsu). He was the second son of Xiao Ni 蕭嶷 (444–492), Prince Wenxian 文獻 of Yuzhang, who was the second son of Xiao Daocheng 蕭道成 (427–482), the found
2 CONC er of the Southern Qi dynasty. In the early Southern Qi Xiao Zike was given the noble title of Marquis of Nankang 南康 county. In 489, at the age of twelve he composed a ''fu'' on the topic of the “tall pine” to match a piece on the
2 CONC  same topic by Xiao Ziliang 蕭子良 (460–494). His composition received special praise from Wang Jian 王儉 (452–489). His first position was governor of Huailing 淮陵 (modern Jiangsu). During the Jianwu period (494–498) he was appointed g
2 CONC overnor of Wu 吳 commandery (modern Suzhou). In 498, Wang Jingze 王敬則 (435–498) led a revolt in Guiji under the pretext of supporting Xiao Zike. Emperor Ming (r. 483–501) summoned Xiao Zike and seventy of his relatives to the Secret
2 CONC ariat with the intention of having them all executed. The day Xiao Zike arrived at the court the emperor decided not to punish him. He appointed him palace cadet in the household of the heir designate. During the reign of Xiao Bao
2 CONC juan 蕭寶卷 (483–501, r. 498–501) he served as palace attendant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Liang dynasty was founded in 502, Xiao Zike's noble title was reduced to that of viscount. He was appointed left senior administrator in the Ministry of Education. Although Xiao Zike was a member of the Southern Qi ruling
2 CONC  family, the Liang founder Xiao Yan 蕭衍 (464–549) treated him and his brothers favorably. Thus, Zike held a series of important positions including governor of Yongjia 永嘉 (modern Wenzhou), chief minister of the court of imperial en
2 CONC tertainments, and director of the palace library. After serving as governor of Lingling 零陵 (administrative seat modern Yongzhou 永州, Hunan), he returned to the imperial court in 518 where he was appointed cavalier attendant-in-ordi
2 CONC nary and Bulwark General of the State. In 520, he was promoted to chamberlain for the imperial clan. In 522 he served as minister in the section for justice of the Department of State Affairs. The next year he was transferred to t
2 CONC he Ministry of Personnel. In 525 Xiao Zike was appointed supervisor of the household of the heir designate Xiao Tong 蕭統 (501–531). In 528 he was sent out as governor of Wu commandery. He died here in 529 at the age of fifty-two. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In his youth Xiao Zike was an accomplished writer. However, he did not preserve his writings, and none of his works has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
