1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Shen Pu 沈璞 (416-453), ''zi'' Daozhen 道真. Liu Song period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shen Pu's ancestral home was Wukang 武康 in Wuxing 吳興 commandery (west of modern Deqing 德清, Huzhou 湖州, Zhejiang). He is the father of Shen Yue 沈約 (441-513). When the rebel Sun En 孫恩 (d. 402) occupied Guiji 會稽 (modern Shaoxing, Zheji
2 CONC ang) in 399, Shen Pu's grandfather Shen Mufu 沈穆夫 (d. 399) served as Sun's aide. Shen Mufu was killed by the Jin general Liu Laozhi 劉牢之 (d. 402). Shen Muzhi's son Shen Linzi 沈林子 (d. 422), Shen Pu's father, escaped and took refuge i
2 CONC n the mountains until he joined Liu Yu 劉裕 (356-422), the eventual founder of the Song dynasty. Shen Linzi earned wide acclaim for his military achievements before and after the founding of the Song in 420.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shen Pu was Shen Linzhi's youngest son. Although he came from a military family, Shen Pu showed literary inclinations from an early age. He even earned praise from the future Emperor Wen (r. 424-453) as an "unusual child." In 435,
2 CONC  when Wang Shaozhi 王韶之 (n.d.) was serving as governor of Wuxing, he repeatedly invited Shen Pu to serve on his staff, but Shen refused to take up the appointment. Sometime later he did accept a position as recorder under another W
2 CONC uxing native Zhang Shao 張邵 (n.d.). In 440 Shen Pu joined the staff of Liu Jun 劉濬 (429-453), Prince of Shixing. One of Shen Pu's colleagues was Fan Ye 范曄 (398-446). However, the emperor was uneasy about Fan Ye's careless and casual
2 CONC  nature, and he entrusted Shen Pu with supervising the affairs of the young prince. In 446, after Fan Ye was executed, the emperor assigned Shen Pu to take charge of the full administration of the province. At the end of the Yuanj
2 CONC ia era (424-453) Shen Pu was appointed governor of Xuyi 盱眙 (administrative seat Xuyi, northeast of modern Xuyi, Jiangsu). Shen Pu won acclaim when he joined with Zang Zhi 臧質 (d. 454) in repelling an invasion by a Northern Wei army
2 CONC  in 450-451. He was then summoned back to the capital, but was sent out almost immediately as governor of Huainan 淮南 (administrative seat modern Xuancheng, Anhui). When Liu Shao 劉劭 (ca. 426-453) staged a coup against Emperor Wen a
2 CONC nd installed himself as emperor in 453, Shen Pu was suspected of allying with Liu Shao. Liu Shao was overthrown by Liu Jun 劉駿 (430-464), Emperor Xiaowu (r. 453-464), and upon the advice of Yan Jun 顏竣 (d. 459), he ordered Shen Pu e
2 CONC xecuted.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Shen Pu was on the staff of the Prince of Shixing, he began composing the "Jiu gong fu" 舊宮賦 (''Fu'' on the old palace?). After a long period had passed and Shen had not finished the piece, the prince inquired of him why he ha
2 CONC d taken so long. He quickly finished it up and presented to the prince who was impressed with his elegant syle and profound thought. Shen Pu had also written other ''fu'', sacrificial offerings, sevens, laments, eulogies, petition
2 CONC s, encomia, tetrameter and pentameter verse. All of these pieces were lost during the transition between the Song and Qi. Shen Yue was able to retrieve about twenty pieces, but non of these has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT David R. Knechtges
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 沈璞 Shen Pu, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017.  http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/COM-0030
